    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> declared war on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> on 3 September 1939, and after the Phoney War the Germans attacked in the West on 10 May 1940. As the French forces collapsed, Prime Minister Paul Reynaud wanted to continue the war from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-africa">North Africa</a>, but Vice-Premier <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philippe-petain">Philippe Pétain</a> and General Maxime Weygand insisted on an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armistice-of-11-november-1918">armistice</a>. The government retreated to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bordeaux">Bordeaux</a>, Reynaud resigned, and President Albert Lebrun appointed the 84-year-old Pétain prime minister on 16 June 1940. The armistice with Germany was signed on 22 June.</p><p>Germany occupied northern France and the whole Atlantic coast and in effect annexed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alsace">Alsace</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lorraine">Lorraine</a>, leaving about two-fifths of metropolitan France and the empire to the French government. It met in the spa town of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vichy">Vichy</a>, which had many hotels and was never officially declared the capital. On 10 July 1940 the Chamber of Deputies and the Senate, lobbied with promises and threats by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pierre-laval">Pierre Laval</a> and Raphaël Alibert, voted by 569 to 80 to give full powers to Pétain. The Constitutional Acts of 11 and 12 July made him head of the French State, and the name French Republic disappeared from official documents. Most French historians and all postwar French governments have held the vote illegal.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>In 1940 the government took direct control of all production, which was synchronized with German demands, and replaced free trade unions with compulsory state unions. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armistice-of-11-november-1918">armistice</a> made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> pay the costs of the German occupation troops, and it is estimated that France provided 42% of all foreign aid to the German economy. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> held about two million French prisoners of war, and a law of September 1942 in effect deported workers to Germany, where by August 1944 they were 15% of the labour force.</p><p>The Germans seized about 20% of French food production, and farm output fell by half for lack of fuel, fertilizer and workers. The rationing system was stringent and badly mismanaged: the official ration gave starvation-level diets of 1013 or fewer calories a day, and a large black market grew. The National Revolution promoted the patriarchal family, motherhood and traditional <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholicism">Catholicism</a>; government agencies had to fire married women employees, and the media were tightly controlled and spread virulent antisemitism. Women of the Resistance fought beside men, and France gave women the vote in 1944.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armistice-of-11-november-1918">armistice</a> allowed a small Armistice Army in the unoccupied zone, set at 3,768 officers, 15,072 non-commissioned officers and 75,360 men, all volunteers, without tanks and short of motor transport. The colonial forces still had nearly 150,000 men in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> area. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vichy">Vichy</a> defended its empire against both sides. On 3 July 1940 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/royal-navy">Royal Navy</a> attacked a French fleet at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mers-el-kebir">Mers El Kébir</a>. In September 1940 Vichy forces at Dakar drove off Operation Menace, and in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franco-thai-war">Franco-Thai War</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thailand">Thailand</a> attacked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indochina">Indochina</a>.</p><p>British and Commonwealth forces, with Free French troops, took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lebanon">Lebanon</a> in the five-week campaign that ended on 14 July 1941, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madagascar">Madagascar</a> from May to November 1942. When the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allies">Allies</a> invaded French <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-africa">North Africa</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/operation-torch">Operation Torch</a> on 8 November 1942, Vichy forces at first resisted, killing 479 Allied soldiers, before Admiral François Darlan ordered them to stop. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> then occupied the south in Case Anton and disbanded the Armistice Army, and the French fleet was scuttled at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toulon">Toulon</a> on 27 November 1942. From 30 January 1943 the Milice, a paramilitary force under Joseph Darnand, fought the Resistance.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Constitutional Acts of July 1940 gave Pétain all legislative, judicial, administrative, executive and diplomatic powers and the right to name his successor. Pétain never promulgated a new constitution. Democratic liberties were suspended, the "crime of opinion" was restored, critics were arrested, and elective bodies were replaced by nominated ones. Five governments followed: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pierre-laval">Pierre Laval</a>'s first, Pierre-Étienne Flandin's of two months to February 1941, François Darlan's to April 1942, and Laval's again until August 1944.</p><p>The regime's programme, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/revolution-nationale">Révolution nationale</a>, sought an anti-modern counter-revolution against the republican tradition, and Pétain's radio speeches blamed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>'s defeat on the "decadence" of the Third Republic. Scholars disagree whether it was fascist: Stanley G. Payne called it rightist and authoritarian but never fascist, while Roger Griffin described it as para-fascist. Historians also reject the label of puppet state: Robert Paxton found it an autonomous government doing things <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> did not require. Technocrats such as Jean Bichelonne pushed reforms, and René Bousquet unified the police, some of which were kept after the war.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>As soon as it was established, the regime took measures of its own against "undesirables": <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a>, immigrants, Freemasons, Communists, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romani-people">Romani</a>, homosexuals and left-wing activists. Some 15,000 people, mostly Jews, were denaturalized. The Law on the status of Jews of 3 October 1940 excluded Jews from the administration, the armed forces, teaching, law, medicine and other professions, and the next day a law authorized the detention of foreign Jews. Internment camps such as Gurs and Drancy became transit camps for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holocaust">Holocaust</a>.</p><p>In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vel-dhiv-roundup">Vel' d'Hiv Roundup</a> of 16 and 17 July 1942, French police under René Bousquet and Jean Leguay arrested 13,152 Jews, including 4,051 children, whom the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gestapo">Gestapo</a> had not asked for, and sent them through Drancy to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oswiecim">Auschwitz</a>. About 25,000 French Jews and 50,000 foreign Jews were deported; according to Robert Paxton, 76,000 Jews were deported and died in the camps. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vichy">Vichy</a> enacted racial laws in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-africa">North Africa</a> too. In 1995 President Jacques Chirac made the first official admission that the French State had been complicit in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deportation">deportations</a>, and in 2017 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emmanuel-macron">Emmanuel Macron</a> said that "it was indeed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> that organised" the roundup.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The empire slipped away from 1940, as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-equatorial-africa">French Equatorial Africa</a>, Cameroun and the Pacific colonies rallied to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-de-gaulle">Charles de Gaulle</a>'s Free <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. After the Allied landings in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-africa">North Africa</a> and the German occupation of the south in November 1942, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vichy">Vichy</a> kept only residual authority. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allies">Allies</a> landed in Normandy and Provence in 1944, and after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liberation-of-paris">liberation of Paris</a> on 25 August 1944 German forces took Pétain and his ministers to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sigmaringen">Sigmaringen</a>. There Fernand de Brinon led a commission with little or no authority until French forces took the town in April 1945.</p><p>The Provisional Government's Ordinance of 9 August 1944 declared all of Vichy's constitutional acts void, and the Allies recognized de Gaulle's government on 23 October 1944. In the purges, as many as 10,000 people were executed without trial, by the estimate de Gaulle accepted, and the purge commissions condemned about 120,000. Pétain was sentenced to death for treason in 1945, a sentence de Gaulle commuted to life imprisonment; Brinon and Darnand were executed. Later trials convicted Paul Touvier in 1994 and Maurice Papon in 1998. The historian Henry Rousso called Vichy a "past that doesn't pass away".</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vichy">Vichy</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> was the authoritarian French State that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philippe-petain">Marshal Pétain</a> headed after the defeat of 1940. Officially neutral but bound by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armistice-of-11-november-1918">armistice</a>, it collaborated with Nazi <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, persecuted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> and other &quot;undesirables&quot; and helped deport 76,000 Jews to their deaths. Its independence ended with the German occupation of November 1942, and it fell with the liberation in 1944.</em></p>

